---
import DocsLayout from "~/layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---
<DocsLayout active="deployment" title="Deployment" description="Move from a local proof-of-concept to a private, observable redaction boundary that your team can operate.">
  <section class="card border-flame-400/20 bg-flame-500/[0.06] p-6 sm:p-8">
    <div class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-flame-300">The recommended path</div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-100">Start with Docker Compose so the API and Redis wiring are correct. Once your application is integrated, keep the same API contract and move the containers behind your ingress, service identity, durable audit storage, and monitoring.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-[25px] text-ink-0">Choose a deployment tier</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">Start with the smallest boundary that fits the workload. Redis is optional for a single-process service when caching, rate limiting, and jobs are disabled; production authentication and trusted hosts are still required.</p>
    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-3 lg:grid-cols-3">
      {[ ["Simple", "One process, usually regex-only. Good for a controlled service with structured detection; no Redis required when the related features are disabled."], ["Standard", "One or a few processes with the pinned local GLiNER2 model and Redis for rate limits, caching, idempotency, and jobs."], ["Scaled", "Multiple identical replicas behind an ingress with shared durable Redis, consistent model revisions, policies, and secrets."] ].map(([name, body]) => <div class="card p-5"><div class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-ink-0">{name}</div><p class="mt-2 text-[13.5px] leading-[1.6] text-ink-400">{body}</p></div>)}
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[13.5px] text-ink-400">The <code>/v1/jobs</code> path writes accepted work to Redis and hands it to a separate <code>redax-worker</code> process. Scale API replicas and workers independently; durable Redis, lease recovery, and worker drain behavior remain deployment acceptance gates.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-[25px] text-ink-0">1. Run locally</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">This gives a developer a repeatable environment with the redaction API and Redis dependency. The health check confirms the service is reachable before you integrate application code.</p>
    <pre class="code-shell mt-5 overflow-x-auto p-5 text-[13px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-100"><code>{`git clone https://github.com/sachncs/redax.git
cd redax
docker compose up

curl http://localhost:8000/healthz`}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-[25px] text-ink-0">From checkout to launch</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">Use this sequence when you need a deployment a developer or operator can execute without reverse-engineering the repository. The first four steps are local and repeatable; the remaining steps become platform work for a private host or Kubernetes cluster.</p>
    <div class="mt-5 grid gap-3 lg:grid-cols-2">
      {[
        ["01", "Prerequisites", "Install Docker Compose v2 and Python 3.13 for local tooling. For the reference topology, also have kubectl, a Kubernetes context, an ingress controller, and a secret manager."],
        ["02", "Clone the reviewed release", "Clone the repository, check out the exact reviewed commit or release tag, and verify CI/security evidence before production. Do not deploy an unreviewed working tree."],
        ["03", "Configure environment", "Start from .env.example for local use. Set REDAX_ENV, REDAX_TRUSTED_HOSTS, REDAX_API_KEYS, REDAX_HASH_SALT, and a valid policy before accepting non-local traffic."],
        ["04", "Configure Redis", "Use the local Compose Redis for development. Production jobs, idempotency, rate limits, cache, and worker coordination require a private TLS Redis endpoint in REDAX_REDIS_URL."],
        ["05", "Configure secrets", "Generate separate API, hash-salt, job-encryption, and audit-integrity values. Inject them through the platform secret manager; do not place them in Git, images, or browser code."],
        ["06", "Configure telemetry", "Choose the approved log, metrics, and OTLP destinations. Configure REDAX_OTLP_ENDPOINT and collectors so metadata is observable without exporting request values."],
        ["07", "Choose hosting and region", "Select Docker on a private host or the checked-in Kubernetes topology. Record region, data residency, node sizing, identity, egress, and ownership; Redax does not choose a cloud provider for you."],
        ["08", "Configure domain and DNS", "Create the DNS record for the ingress or reverse proxy and set REDAX_TRUSTED_HOSTS to the exact public hostname. Keep internal health and metrics routes private."],
        ["09", "Configure TLS", "Issue a certificate for the public hostname and configure renewal. Kubernetes users create redax-tls; private-host users terminate HTTPS at their approved proxy."],
        ["10", "Deploy", "Run docker compose up -d --build for the local-like stack, or kubectl apply -k deploy/kubernetes for the reference topology after creating its Redis, API, and TLS secrets."],
        ["11", "Check setup state", "There are currently no application database migrations. Redis namespace, policy files, model revision, and secret versions are deployment inputs that must be recorded with the release."],
        ["12", "Verify health", "Check /healthz for process liveness and /readyz after Redis/model initialization. Route traffic only after readiness is healthy."],
        ["13", "Verify the API", "Send a synthetic authenticated POST to /v1/redact and confirm the response contains a replacement and not the source marker. Repeat an idempotency request where that workflow is enabled."],
        ["14", "Verify the frontend/demo", "Open the website demo to understand browser-local regex behavior. It is not a production API canary and does not validate your server model or ingress."],
        ["15", "Configure backups", "Set managed Redis persistence, backup cadence, restore owner, and RPO/RTO. For local development, use the documented RDB snapshot commands; do not treat them as managed HA evidence."],
        ["16", "Configure monitoring", "Scrape /metrics, alert on readiness, 5xx/503/504, latency, queue age, Redis, audit, and telemetry failures, and protect metrics and audit endpoints."],
        ["17", "Validate the launch gate", "Run the synthetic canary and failure drills in the production launch runbook: replica loss, worker loss, Redis failover, traffic ramp, rollout, rollback, telemetry privacy, and recovery."],
      ].map(([number, title, body]) => <article class="card p-5 sm:flex sm:gap-5"><div class="shrink-0 font-mono text-[12px] text-flame-300">{number}</div><div><h3 class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-ink-0">{title}</h3><p class="mt-2 text-[13.5px] leading-[1.6] text-ink-400">{body}</p></div></article>)}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-[25px] text-ink-0">2. Set the production boundary</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">The local Compose profile is intentionally marked as development. Production startup requires API keys and trusted hosts, and the service does not enable browser CORS unless you configure it.</p>
    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-3">
      {[ ["REDAX_ENV", "Set prod for deployed traffic; only dev permits unauthenticated local development."], ["REDAX_API_KEYS", "Required in prod. Keep keys in your secret manager and rotate them."], ["REDAX_TRUSTED_HOSTS", "Required in prod. List the public hostnames accepted by the service."], ["REDAX_CORS_ORIGINS", "Leave empty unless browser clients need access; list exact origins, never a wildcard in prod."], ["REDAX_HASH_SALT", "Use a unique random value per deployment so hashes and cache keys cannot be correlated across environments."], ["REDAX_REDIS_URL", "Point rate limiting, idempotency, response caching, and jobs at durable Redis."], ["REDAX_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES", "Bound buffered response amplification; streaming responses retain their own chunk and duration limits."], ["REDAX_AUDIT_PATH", "Mount durable storage or a log shipper. The audit file contains metadata, not raw PII."], ["REDAX_OTLP_ENDPOINT", "Send traces to your internal collector when you need distributed request visibility."] ].map(([name, body]) => <div class="card p-5 sm:flex sm:items-start sm:gap-6"><code class="shrink-0 text-flame-200">{name}</code><p class="mt-2 sm:mt-0 text-[14px] text-ink-300">{body}</p></div>)}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-[25px] text-ink-0">3. Probe the right things</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
      {[ ["/healthz", "Liveness", "Is the process running? Use this for restart decisions."], ["/readyz", "Readiness", "Is the redactor initialized and able to serve traffic? Use this for load balancers."], ["/metrics", "Metrics", "Are latency, errors, queue depth, and detected entities within your operating range?"] ].map(([path, title, body]) => <div class="card p-5"><code class="text-flame-200">{path}</code><div class="mt-3 text-[15px] font-semibold text-ink-0">{title}</div><p class="mt-2 text-[13px] text-ink-400">{body}</p></div>)}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-[25px] text-ink-0">4. Apply the reference production topology</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">The checked-in Kubernetes manifests are a real starting point for a private deployment, but they deliberately stop at the application boundary. You choose the cloud, region, node policy, managed Redis service, backup policy, telemetry destination, DNS provider, and certificate issuer during deployment because those are environment ownership decisions—not portable Redax defaults.</p>
    <div class="mt-4 card border-flame-400/20 bg-flame-500/[0.06] p-5">
      <div class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-flame-300">Before you run this</div>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[14px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-200">Have a Kubernetes context pointed at the intended production cluster, a TLS Redis endpoint with its backup/failover policy recorded, an ingress controller, a certificate chain, and a secret manager ready. Replace every example value below; never commit generated credentials or a production secret.</p>
    </div>
    <pre class="code-shell mt-5 overflow-x-auto p-5 text-[13px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-100"><code>{`# From a reviewed Redax release checkout
kubectl config current-context
kubectl create namespace redax

# Store API/authentication and encryption material outside Git.
kubectl -n redax create secret generic redax-api \\
  --from-literal=REDAX_API_KEYS='replace-with-a-scoped-key' \\
  --from-literal=REDAX_HASH_SALT='replace-with-a-unique-random-value' \\
  --from-literal=job_payload_encryption_key="$(python3.13 -c 'from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; print(Fernet.generate_key().decode())')" \\
  --from-literal=audit_integrity_key='replace-with-independent-random-secret'

# Use your managed Redis TLS endpoint; Redis is not installed by these manifests.
kubectl -n redax create secret generic redax-redis \\
  --from-literal=url='rediss://redis.example.internal:6380/0'

# Use a certificate issued for the production hostname.
kubectl -n redax create secret tls redax-tls \\
  --cert=/path/to/fullchain.pem --key=/path/to/privkey.pem

kubectl apply -k deploy/kubernetes
kubectl -n redax rollout status deployment/redax-api
kubectl -n redax rollout status deployment/redax-worker`}</code></pre>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[13.5px] text-ink-400">Then configure the ingress hostname and DNS record, apply your certificate renewal mechanism, connect Prometheus/custom metrics to the HPA, and create alerts for readiness failures, error rate, latency, queue age, Redis health, audit delivery, and backup failures. See <a href="/redax/docs/production/" class="text-flame-200 underline decoration-flame-500/40 underline-offset-4">production configuration</a> for the deployment decisions that must be recorded.</p>
    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {[ ["This proves", "The manifests can be applied, the API and worker rollouts become ready, and the cluster can reach the configured dependencies."], ["This does not prove", "Provider HA, Redis failover, backups/restores, DNS/TLS renewal, telemetry delivery, capacity, or your SLOs. Run those checks in the target environment before launch." ] ].map(([title, body]) => <div class="card p-5"><div class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-flame-300">{title}</div><p class="mt-3 text-[13.5px] leading-[1.6] text-ink-300">{body}</p></div>)}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-[25px] text-ink-0">Production checklist</h2>
    <ul class="mt-4 grid gap-3 text-[14px] text-ink-200">
      {["Authentication is enabled and tested with an invalid key.", "The API is reachable only from the services that need the privacy boundary.", "Redis and audit storage survive a container restart.", "Readiness, error rate, latency, and queue depth are monitored.", "A policy version is committed with the application release.", "The team has verified that logs, traces, cache keys, and audit events do not contain raw values."].map((item) => <li class="card flex gap-3 p-4"><span class="text-sage-400">✓</span><span>{item}</span></li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
